this year Rainbow six siege in  battlefield hardline are both tackling  hostage rescue modes one with a fast  eSports focus and the other as a  tactical shooter with destructible  buildings not the battlefield one I set  out to research what makes a good  hostage rescue game only to find out  there aren't really a lot of them out  there it's a tough thing to pull off in  PvP because usually one side has an  unfair advantage breathing worse nobody  cares about the hostages and matches  devolve in the team deathmatch here's  some lessons learned from past hostage  games first let's go back to the idea of  hostages as a game mechanic why even  have them traditionally the idea of a  hostage is to slow down a response or as  a bargaining chip in game terms this  hopefully translates into a sense of  tension attackers should be scared and  cautious and turn into defender  territory if nobody cares about the  hostage then you don't really have a  hostage mode you just have a building  entry game so number one both teams have  to have an invested interest in the  hostages safety a good example of this  is in the co-op title payday 2 where  players taking and keeping hostages safe  vastly reduces the difficulty and police  response amateur burglars would blast  their way through levels and then  struggle to survive as increasingly  dangerous police waves showed up to take  them down  but veteran payday 2 players through  unlocked tactics and the keeping help of  zip ties might never have to fire a shot  payday 2 did a great job of tying  hostage treatment to ease or difficulty  of a win early builds of counter-strike  also had issues with hostage value where  terrorists simply found it easier to  manage with fewer and would execute half  for more at the outside of rounds both  of these titles have led me to believe  hostage rescue modes need an instant  fail scenario or heavy penalties for  either side if the hostage is killed it  makes sense conceptually the entire  point of the attackers is to rescue the  entire point of the defenders should be  to escape I'd like to go further and see  the extractions on either side lead to a  win not just the police having to  extract which doesn't give the defenders  any incentive to evade number two tools  for Team tactics are gonna be critical  sure Ghost Recon Future Soldier had some  great sync shotting in single-player but  how do you pull that off with real  players SWAT 4 had some good ideas in  its single-player campaign  the commander led two squads on each  assault and not only gave orders but  look through the helmet cams of his or  her subordinates the commander called  out what kind of preaching and clearing  to do and ultimately led to a unified  tactical response on the flipside for  the defenders that would make the  commander a very juicy high-value target  Ghost Recon phantoms also has a good  solution for team unity with players  being aware of other friendly players  through a friend tracking system that  shows up in the form of lines between a  player and their teammates another way  to encourage team cohesion is gear  splitting if only one guy has the  breaching charges and one guy has the  grappling hook  everyone needs a rally to them to  accomplish tasks I'd love to see a game  where say the bolt cutters were on the  players back and another player would  have to pull it off them to use it so  far we've seen some of these elements in  hardline in siege hardline was spotting  in contextual commands and siege with  gear equipment and voice chat but I am  worried that both of these games hostage  modes will devolve as all players run  off and try to lone-wolf the maps and  that brings us to our last point number  three replayability nothing makes a game  more of playable than a large set of  tools and some randomization payday 2  randomizes doors crates even entire  ships on some levels players are  familiar with the general layout of maps  but they can run into any number of  unexpected barriers SWAT 4 randomize bad  guy placement and bad guys were also  capable of freely moving through levels  once the action started some of this  unpredictability will come naturally  from PvP encounters but environmental  changes would add a lot more content in  terms of gear SWAT 4 had it right  attackers had a wide range of lethal and  non-lethal options grenades and gadgets  there was nothing more entertaining and  also hilarious than running into a room  with a paintball gun full of pepper  spray and shouting get down get down or  you know from doing it boys brutality  ultimately we're gonna see this year  hostage-rescue games and modes are still  appealing I've got great memories from  America's Army - snake plane coop  mission and others in the GameSpot  office recall hunted from team fortress  what would you like to see an either  Rainbow six siege or hardlines rescue  mode leave your thoughts in the comments  below and stay tuned to game spot comm  for continuing coverage of Rainbow six  siege and battlefield hardline
 

